e does not have any immediate winning moves (like a "Four" or "Open Three") to counter-attack, so blocking is the only viable option.\n\n**Reason:** Black has a "Four"\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current board state shows a critical threat from the opponent (Black). Black has formed a horizontal line of four stones on Row 9 at coordinates D9, E9, F9, and G9. This constitutes a "Four" (or "Closed Four" since H9 is blocked by White). The left side of this line at C9 is open. If White does not play at C9 immediately, Black will place a stone there on the next turn to complete a 5-in-a-row and win the game. While there were potential diagonal threats (E8-H11 and H8-E11), these are already blocked on both ends by White\'s stones at D7/I12 and I7/D12 respectively. White has no immediate offensive winning combination (VCF) to ignore this threat, making the block mandatory.\n\nReason: To block the opponent\'s immediate winning threat of a 4-in-a-row on Row 9 (D9, E9, F9, G9) which would become a 5-in-a-row if Black plays at C9.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "C", "row": 9}'}
